# 000300-000399/20060911T231205Z_75182047.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=1bfc|+Addition lines are ones that should be added to the original file to obtain the new file. Deletion lines are ones that should be deleted from the original file to obtain the new file.%0A%0AThe contextual lines are optional, but generally surround the addition and/or deletion lines, traditionally 3 at the beginning and 3 at the end of each chunk. These are lines that have not changed between the two files, and so can be used as a reference to locate the chunk's place in the file. Providing context with such lines is useful both for human [[readability]] & reference, and for any [[patch]] program trying to apply said diff to a slightly modified version of the original file. If, say, lines have been inserted before a chunk in the original file, the chunkrange's [[line number]]s (see below) will likely be incorrect, yet the contextual lines may allow the patch program to find the correct location to add/remove the chunk's lines. If two chunks occur very close to one another or 'overlap', they should be merged into one single chunk and contextual lines may be inserted into the middle of the chunk to reflect this.%0A%0AThe format of the range information line is as follows:%0A @@ %2Dchunkrange %2Bchunkrange @@%0A%0AA 'chunkrange' is of the format '''line_number,chunk_size'''. The two chunkranges pertain to the original file (%2Dchunkrange) and the new file (%2Bchunkrange). Each should specify the location of the first chunk line in the relevant file (line_number), and the number of lines in the chunk (chunk_size) ''pertaining to the relevant file'' %2D in other words, the %2Dchunkrange should be a sum of all contextual and deletion chunk lines, and the %2Bchunkrange should be a sum of all contextual and addition chunk lines. The contextual lines obviously appear in both files (as by definition they haven't changed), and so should be included in both chunk sizes. If either chunk size doesn't correspond correctly with the number of actual such lines in the chunk, or any lines in the chunk are not preceded by %2B, %2D, or space, then the diff file is invalid.%0A%0AIn practice, this format makes the diff files more easily human%2Dreadable. Generally, a line preceded by a %2B can be read as 'added to file', one preceded by a %2D can be read as 'removed from file', and one preceded by a space can be read as 'stayed the same'. If a line is only modified, it will simply be listed as if the old line had been removed and the new one added. As both such entries will appear adjacent to one another, it's usually quite obvious if the line has only been modified. An obvious occurance of this in the example below is:%0A %2Dcheck this dokument. On%0A %2Bcheck this document. On%0A%0AUnified format is usually invoked using the "%2Du" [[command line option]]. This output is often used as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program.%0A%0A|=2478|
# 000300-000399/20060912T032959Z_75220087.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=c96|+ [[Angle bracket]]s appear at the beginning of lines that are added or deleted.  Addition lines are added to the original file to appear in the new file. Deletion lines are deleted from the original file to be missing in the new file.%0A%0A|=35|+ |=3e9c|
