# 000300-000399/20070118T174443Z_101596123.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=4ec|-9|=c|-9|=be|-9|=21c|-9|=291|-9|=4|-9|=b26|-9|=4|-9|=44|-9|=21|-9|=e66|-9|=4|-9|=34|-9|=18|-9|=26b7|
# 000300-000399/20070118T175412Z_101597850.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=4a4|-1a|+A|=1|-1|+y|=1|-2|=1|-2|+f|=5|-4|=1|-d|+xamp|=4|-7|=2|-11|=3|-2|+e|=3|+ticl|=5|-4|=f|-e|+two|=6|+,|=1|-3|=1|-1|+''|=1|-3|=1|-4|=1|-8|+g|=1|-2|+nal''|=1|-4|=4|-10|+''n|=1|-1d|+w'':|=4ae|-17|=4a6|+ History |=1|+%3D%0A%0AThe '''diff''' program was developed in the early 1970s on the [[Unix]] operating system which was emerging from [[AT&T]] [[Bell Labs]] in Murray Hill, New Jersey.  The final version, first shipped with the 5th Edition of Unix in 1974, was entirely written by [[Douglas McIlroy]].  This research was published in a 1976 paper co%2Dwritten with [[James W. Hunt]] who developed an initial prototype of diff.%0A%0AMcIlroy's work was preceded and influenced by [[Stephen|=2|+. J|=1|+h|=1|+son%7CS|=2|+ve Johnson]]'s comparison program on [[GECOS]] and [[Mike Lesk]]'s '''proof''' program.  '''Proof''' originated on Uni|=1|+ and produced line%2Dby%2Dline changes like diff and even used angle%2Dbracke|=1|+s (">" and "<")|=4|+ presenting line insertions and deletions in the progra|=1|+'s output.  The [[heuristic]]s used in these e|=1|+rly applications were, however, deemed unreliable.  The potential usefulness of a diff tool provoked McIlroy into researching and designing a more robust tool that could be used in a variety of tasks but perform well in the processing and space limitations of the [[PDP%2D11]]'s hardware.  His approach resulted from collaboration also with individuals at Bell Labs including [[Alfred Aho]], [[Elliot Pinson]], [[Jeffrey Ullman]], and [[Harold S. Stone]].%0A%0AIn the context of Unix, the use of the [[Ed (Unix)%7Ced]] line editor provided diff with the natural ability to create machine%2Dusable "edit scripts".  These edit scripts, when saved to a file, can, along with the original file, be reconstituted by '''ed''' into the modified file in its entirety.  This greatly reduced the space necessary to maintain multiple versions of a file.  McIlroy considered writing a post%2Dprocessor for diff where a variety of output formats could be designed and implemented, but he found it more frugal and simpler to have diff be responsible for generating the syntax and reverse%2Dorder input accepted by the '''ed''' command.%0AIn 1985, [[Larry Wall]] composed a separate utility, [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]], that generalized and extended the ability to modify files with diff output.%0A%0AIn diff's early years, common uses included comparing changes in the source of software code and markup for technical documents, verifying program debugging output, comparing filesystem listings and analyzing computer assembly code.  The output targeted for '''ed''' was motivated to provide compression for a sequence of modifications made to a file.  The [[SCCS%7CSource Code Control System]] (SCCS) emerged in the late 1970s as a direct consequence of this development.%0A%0AA conceptual predecessor of diff includes [[Project Xanadu]], a hypertex|=2|+project established in 1960 that had envisioned a version tracking system necessary for its "transpointing windows" feature.  As part of this feature, file differences were subsumed in the expansive term "[[transclusion]]", when a document has included in it parts of other documents or revisions.%0A%0AIn the digital realm of the humanities, computer comparison systems were understood to have been created for working on literary works published as large volumes.%0A%0A|=2|+ Variations %3D|=3|-1|+Most diff implementations remain outwardly unchanged since 1975.  The modifications include improvements to the core algorithm, the addition of useful features to the command, and the design of new output formats. The basic algorithm is described in the papers ''An O(ND) Difference Algorithm and its Variations'' by Eugene W. Myers and in ''A File Comparison Program'' by Webb Miller and Myers.  The algorithm was independently discovered and described in ''Algorithms for Approximate String Matching'', by [[E. Ukkonen]].  The first editions of the diff program were designed for line comparisons of text files expecting the [[newline]] character to delimit lines.  By the 1980s, support for binary files resulted in a shift in the application's design and implementation.%0A%0APostprocessors '''sdiff''' and '''diffmk''' re|=1|+der side%2Dby%2Dside diff listings and applied change marks to printed documents, respectively.  Both were developed elsewhere in Bell Labs in or before 1981.%0A%0AThe [[BSD%7CBerkeley distribution of Unix]] made a point of adding|=17|+ (%2DC) and the ability to recurse on filesystem directory structures (%2Dr), adding those features in 2.8 BSD, released in July 1981.  The context format of diff introduced at Berkeley helped with distributing patches for source code that may have been changed minimally.%0A%0A[[Diff3]] compares one file against two other files.  It was originally developed by [[Paul Jensen]] to reconcile changes made by two persons editing a common source.  It is seldom invoked directly and is largely subsumed by the [[merge (Unix)%7Cmerge]] program.  However, it is used internally by many [[revision control]] systems.%0A%0A[http://www.gnu.org/software/wdiff/wdiff.html Wdiff] makes it easy to see the words or phrases that changed in a text document, especially in the presence of word%2Dwrapping or different column widths. [http://hpux.cs.utah.edu/hppd/hpux/Text/spiff%2D1.0 Spiff] goes yet further, ignoring floating point differences under a tunable precision and ignoring irrelevancies in program files such as whitespace and comment formatting.%0A%0A%3D%3D%3D Context format %3D%3D%3D%0A%0AIn the context format|=d04|-1|+Unified context diffs were originally developed by [[Wayne Davison]] in August 1990 (in '''unidiff''' which appeared in Volume 14 of comp.sources.misc).  [[Richard Stallman]] added unified diff support to the [[GNU%7CGNU Project]]'s diff utility one month later, and the feature debuted in '''GNU diff''' 1.15, released in January 1991. GNU diff has since generalized the context format to allow arbitrary formatting of diffs.  GNU diff and diff3 are included in the '''diffutils''' package with other diff and [[Patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] related utilities.  [[Emacs]] has [[Ediff]] for showing the changes a patch would provide in a user interface that combines interactive editing and merging capabilities for patch files.%0A%0A%0AThe forma|=bc1|-11a2|=229|-5c4|=9f3|
# 000300-000399/20070118T175459Z_101597988.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=2e7|-b6c|=bfc|+.%0A%0A%3D%3D Usage %3D%3D%0A%0AIt is invoked from the command line with the names of two files: <tt>diff ''original''  ''new''</tt>.  The output of the command represents the changes required to make the ''original'' file become the ''new'' file.%0A%0AIf ''original'' and ''new'' are directories, then diff will be run on each file that exists in both directories.  An option, <tt>%2Dr</tt>, will descend any matching subdirectories to compare files between directories.%0A%0AAny of the examples in the article use the following two files,  ''original'' and ''new'':%0A%0A{%7C class%3D"prettytable"%0A%7C%2D valign%3D"top"%0A%7C%0A''original'':%0A%0A  This part of the%0A  document has stayed the%0A  same from version to%0A  version.  It shouldn't%0A  be shown if it doesn't%0A  change.  Otherwise, that%0A  would not be helping to%0A  compress the size of the%0A  changes.%0A  %0A  This paragraph contains%0A  text that is outdated.%0A  It will be deleted in the%0A  near future.%0A  %0A  It is important to spell%0A  check this dokument. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A  Nothing in the rest of%0A  this paragraph needs to%0A  be changed. Things can%0A  be added after it.%0A%7C %0A''new'':%0A%0A  This is an important%0A  notice! It should%0A  therefore be located at%0A  the beginning of this%0A  document!%0A  %0A  This part of the%0A  document has stayed the%0A  same from version to%0A  version.  It shouldn't%0A  be shown if it doesn't%0A  change.  Otherwise, that%0A  would not be helping to%0A  compress anything.%0A  %0A  It is important to spell%0A  check this document. On%0A  the other hand, a%0A  misspelled word isn't%0A  the end of the world.%0A  Nothing in the rest of%0A  this paragraph needs to%0A  be changed. Things can%0A  be added after it.%0A  %0A  This paragraph contains%0A  important new additions%0A  to this document.%0A%7C}%0A%0AThe command <tt>diff original new</tt> produces the following ''normal diff output'':%0A%0A  0a1,6%0A  > This is an important%0A  > notice! It should%0A  > therefore be located at%0A  > the beginning of this%0A  > document!%0A  >%0A  8,14c14%0A  < compress the size of the%0A  < changes.%0A  <%0A  < This paragraph contains%0A  < text that is outdated.%0A  < It will be deleted in the%0A  < near future.%0A  %2D%2D%2D%0A  > compress anything.%0A  17c17%0A  < check this dokument. On%0A  %2D%2D%2D%0A  > check this document. On%0A  24a25,28%0A  >%0A  > This paragraph contains%0A  > important new additions%0A  > to this document.%0A%0AIn this traditional output format, '''a''' stands for ''added'', '''d''' for ''deleted'' and '''c''' for ''changed''.  Line numbers of the original file appear before a/d/c and those of the modified file appear after.  [[Angle bracket]]s appear at the beginning of lines that are added, deleted or changed.  Addition lines are added to the original file to appear in the new file. Deletion lines are deleted from the original file to be missing in the new file.%0A%0ABy default, lines common to both files are not shown.  Lines that have moved will show up as added on their new location and as deleted on their old location|=3029|
